Before — a new discount type means a new subclass, and pricing logic is scattered across a hierarchy:

```java
class Order { double total() { return basePrice; } }
class DiscountedOrder extends Order {
    @Override double total() { return basePrice * 0.9; }
}
```

After — discount logic is composed in as a swappable object, no hierarchy required:

```java
interface DiscountStrategy { double apply(double basePrice); }

class NoDiscount implements DiscountStrategy {
    public double apply(double basePrice) { return basePrice; }
}
class TenPercentOff implements DiscountStrategy {
    public double apply(double basePrice) { return basePrice * 0.9; }
}

class Order {
    private final DiscountStrategy discount;
    Order(DiscountStrategy discount) { this.discount = discount; }
    double total() { return discount.apply(basePrice); }
}
```

Adding a new discount now means writing one small `DiscountStrategy` implementation — `Order` itself never changes, and any `Order` can be handed a different strategy at construction time.
